1
00:00:03,420 --> 00:00:06,190
I was reading about this jellyfish that never dies.

2
00:00:06,190 --> 00:00:08,030
Instead, it reverts to its asexual state

3
00:00:08,030 --> 00:00:09,540
and then grows up again.

4
00:00:09,540 --> 00:00:11,170
We thought my 90-year-old grandfather

5
00:00:11,170 --> 00:00:12,720
had reverted to an asexual state,

6
00:00:12,720 --> 00:00:14,600
but the lawsuit from his Jamaican caregiver

7
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:16,630
proved us all wrong.

8
00:00:17,100 --> 00:00:19,640
My point is, immortality is not only a possibility,

9
00:00:19,640 --> 00:00:20,600
it is real.

10
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:21,900
Only if you're this jellyfish

11
00:00:21,900 --> 00:00:23,840
which periodically reverts to a mass of

12
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:26,050
undifferentiated protoplasm.

13
00:00:26,050 --> 00:00:27,780
If I could keep my Gmail account,

14
00:00:27,780 --> 00:00:29,870
I'd be okay with that.

15
00:00:30,620 --> 00:00:32,510
Hello, fellas.

16
00:00:32,940 --> 00:00:35,250
Remember when we were trying to figure out what that smell

17
00:00:35,250 --> 00:00:37,590
coming from Professor Tupperman's office was?

18
00:00:37,590 --> 00:00:40,740
-Yeah? - Turns out it was Tupperman.

19
00:00:41,460 --> 00:00:44,390
Dead at his desk for two weeks.

20
00:00:44,390 --> 00:00:45,480
- Oh, my God. - That's terrible.

21
00:00:45,480 --> 00:00:48,040
In lieu of flowers, the department chair

22
00:00:48,040 --> 00:00:51,530
is asking that everyone donate a bottle of Febreze.

23
00:00:52,350 --> 00:00:54,420
If we're going to change the topic from jellyfish,

24
00:00:54,510 --> 00:00:56,040
I'd be okay with brine shrimp

25
00:00:56,040 --> 00:00:58,370
or the history of the unicycle.

26
00:00:59,110 --> 00:01:01,780
Show a little compassion-- a man died.

27
00:01:01,780 --> 00:01:03,780
And turned into a puddle of goo.

28
00:01:03,780 --> 00:01:06,080
Now, we can either sit around

29
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:08,590
 and cry over spilt professor, or...

30
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:10,760
we can rejoice in the knowledge

31
00:01:10,760 --> 00:01:13,620
that a tenured position has just opened up.

32
00:01:14,070 --> 00:01:15,880
I choose to do the latter.

33
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:19,770
Excuse me, the whole tenure system is ridiculous.

34
00:01:19,770 --> 00:01:21,410
A guaranteed job for life

35
00:01:21,410 --> 00:01:23,800
only encourages the faculty to become complacent.

36
00:01:23,800 --> 00:01:25,780
If we really want science to advance,

37
00:01:25,780 --> 00:01:29,290
people should have chips implanted in their skulls

38
00:01:29,290 --> 00:01:32,100
that explode when they say something stupid.

39
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:35,890
Well, I believe people do their best work

40
00:01:35,890 --> 00:01:38,140
when they feel safe and secure.

41
00:01:42,440 --> 00:01:45,110
If you need my nose, you'll find it firmly lodged

42
00:01:45,110 --> 00:01:47,660
up the rectum of the tenure committee.

43
00:01:49,150 --> 00:01:51,960
You Ph.D's gonna go suck up like Kripke?

44
00:01:51,960 --> 00:01:52,620
No.

45
00:01:52,620 --> 00:01:53,700
I mean, I'll apply,

46
00:01:53,700 --> 00:01:55,910
but I'm not gonna stoop to playing politics.

47
00:01:55,910 --> 00:01:56,930
Yeah, me neither.

48
00:01:56,930 --> 00:01:58,080
It should be about the work.

49
00:01:58,080 --> 00:01:59,230
And if I can't get tenure,

50
00:01:59,230 --> 00:02:01,230
I'd like to see you or Sheldon get it.

51
00:02:01,230 --> 00:02:03,370
Raj, don't dangle false hope

52
00:02:03,370 --> 00:02:05,490
in front of Leonard like that.

53
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:07,250
Excuse me,

54
00:02:07,250 --> 00:02:09,720
but I think I'm just as qualified as you are.

55
00:02:40,450 --> 00:02:43,580
- So "tenured" means a job for life? - Yup.

56
00:02:43,580 --> 00:02:44,550
And you can't get fired

57
00:02:44,550 --> 00:02:45,650
even if you're bad at it?

58
00:02:45,650 --> 00:02:47,180
Mm, not really.

59
00:02:47,180 --> 00:02:49,600
Wow, sounds a lot like being a pretty waitress

60
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:51,730
at the Cheesecake Factory.

61
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:55,320
While I disagree with the premise of tenure,

62
00:02:55,360 --> 00:02:56,620
if they gave it to me,

63
00:02:56,630 --> 00:02:58,260
it wouldn't diminish my output.

64
00:02:58,260 --> 00:03:00,230
You know, I'm like the sun.

65
00:03:00,230 --> 00:03:02,430
Can't turn this off.

66
00:03:04,740 --> 00:03:07,170
Are Rajesh and Leonard competing for it as well?

67
00:03:08,300 --> 00:03:09,520
Do they know they don't stand a chance

68
00:03:09,520 --> 00:03:11,250
'cause you're so great?

69
00:03:11,920 --> 00:03:13,600
Well put.

70
00:03:14,540 --> 00:03:16,240
You know, I must say I go back and forth

71
00:03:16,240 --> 00:03:17,820
on this boyfriend-girlfriend thing,

72
00:03:17,820 --> 00:03:19,460
but those moments when you worship me

73
00:03:19,460 --> 00:03:21,390
really keep you in the running.

74
00:03:25,130 --> 00:03:26,710
It would be nice to have the increased income.

75
00:03:26,710 --> 00:03:28,310
Stop taking money from my parents.

76
00:03:28,310 --> 00:03:30,840
Money from family does come with strings attached.

77
00:03:30,840 --> 00:03:33,080
You have no idea.

78
00:03:33,080 --> 00:03:36,020
They buy me a new BMW for my birthday

79
00:03:36,020 --> 00:03:37,430
but can I get seat warmers?

80
00:03:37,430 --> 00:03:39,110
No.

81
00:03:39,930 --> 00:03:41,410
"Rajesh, if you want a warm butt,

82
00:03:41,410 --> 00:03:42,740
you're gonna have to pay for it yourself."

83
00:03:42,740 --> 00:03:44,870
Well, maybe I will, old man!

84
00:03:47,850 --> 00:03:50,270
I think that's enough wine for now.

85
00:03:52,010 --> 00:03:53,910
I just keep thinking about how cool it would be

86
00:03:53,910 --> 00:03:55,500
if I called my mom and told her

87
00:03:55,500 --> 00:03:57,710
that I got tenure at Caltech.

88
00:03:57,710 --> 00:03:58,820
She'd be proud, huh?

89
00:03:58,820 --> 00:03:59,820
Oh, very.

90
00:03:59,820 --> 00:04:02,410
Assuming she takes my call.

91
00:04:04,080 --> 00:04:06,850
My primary concern with being granted tenure

92
00:04:06,850 --> 00:04:10,160
is living with an insanely jealous roommate after the fact.

93
00:04:10,160 --> 00:04:13,120
I might need to sleep with a gun under my pillow.

94
00:04:13,120 --> 00:04:15,160
Or a chainsaw.

95
00:04:15,820 --> 00:04:18,970
Or you take advantage of your newfound economic stability

96
00:04:18,970 --> 00:04:20,260
and move out...

97
00:04:20,260 --> 00:04:21,940
buy a house...

98
00:04:21,940 --> 00:04:23,480
get married...

99
00:04:23,480 --> 00:04:25,340
start a family.

100
00:04:26,830 --> 00:04:28,220
Yeah, or...

101
00:04:28,220 --> 00:04:30,580
the chainsaw.

102
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:35,640
And I will return to New Delhi in triumph

103
00:04:35,690 --> 00:04:38,760
atop a bejeweled white elephant!

104
00:04:39,870 --> 00:04:41,910
And you know what will be on that elephant's back?

105
00:04:41,910 --> 00:04:44,140
A seat warmer.

106
00:04:45,170 --> 00:04:48,250
"No, Father, you may not have a ride!"

107
00:04:50,730 --> 00:04:52,470
I'm gonna make some coffee.

108
00:04:54,330 --> 00:04:56,670
So, who do you have to schmooze to get this deal?

109
00:04:56,670 --> 00:04:58,020
I'm not gonna schmooze anybody.

110
00:04:58,020 --> 00:05:00,120
I'm gonna let my work speak for itself.

111
00:05:00,120 --> 00:05:01,110
That's great.

112
00:05:01,110 --> 00:05:02,500
That shows a lot of integrity.

113
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:03,910
Thank you.

114
00:05:04,520 --> 00:05:06,630
I'm a naive idiot, right?

115
00:05:07,390 --> 00:05:09,130
Oh, good, you heard me.

116
00:05:10,560 --> 00:05:11,830
You know, if you really want tenure,

117
00:05:11,830 --> 00:05:14,300
maybe you should cozy up to the people making the decision.

118
00:05:14,300 --> 00:05:17,320
Sheldon Cooper does not do "cozy."

119
00:05:17,810 --> 00:05:19,850
You don't say.

120
00:05:21,180 --> 00:05:22,650
But I suppose it wouldn't hurt

121
00:05:22,650 --> 00:05:24,540
to know who's on the committee.

122
00:05:24,540 --> 00:05:25,750
Let's see...

123
00:05:25,750 --> 00:05:27,560
Oh, Janine Davis.

124
00:05:27,560 --> 00:05:29,110
Oh, dear.

125
00:05:29,110 --> 00:05:30,670
Is that a problem?

126
00:05:32,470 --> 00:05:33,450
Yeah, even you.

127
00:05:33,450 --> 00:05:35,410
You're a slave.

128
00:05:38,930 --> 00:05:41,060
I'm a what?

129
00:05:42,890 --> 00:05:45,230
I'm not sure; it could go either way.

130
00:05:55,710 --> 00:05:58,470
Hey. Mrs. Davis.

131
00:05:59,670 --> 00:06:01,460
Dr. Hofstadter.

132
00:06:01,460 --> 00:06:02,750
Just thought I'd come down

133
00:06:02,750 --> 00:06:05,330
and start getting ready for swimsuit season.

134
00:06:06,820 --> 00:06:07,930
Good for you.

135
00:06:08,550 --> 00:06:09,240
Not that you need it.

136
00:06:09,240 --> 00:06:11,360
I bet you look great in a swimsuit.

137
00:06:12,610 --> 00:06:14,130
Thank you.

138
00:06:14,670 --> 00:06:17,060
I've got what my father used to call "furniture disease."

139
00:06:17,060 --> 00:06:19,280
My chest is falling into my drawers.

140
00:06:25,620 --> 00:06:27,560
I'm not, uh, familiar with this model.

141
00:06:27,560 --> 00:06:29,610
How do I make it start?

142
00:06:29,610 --> 00:06:31,580
You push start.

143
00:06:32,450 --> 00:06:33,780
Right.

144
00:06:41,290 --> 00:06:43,020
This one might be broken.

145
00:06:43,020 --> 00:06:47,060
- You have to move. - Got it.

146
00:06:49,390 --> 00:06:50,440
There.

147
00:06:57,020 --> 00:06:58,390
Oh, yeah!

148
00:06:59,330 --> 00:07:01,980
Yeah, now I'm feeling it.

149
00:07:02,550 --> 00:07:04,010
Oh, this is great.

150
00:07:04,750 --> 00:07:07,230
I could do this for the rest of my life.

151
00:07:07,230 --> 00:07:08,710
Hey, speaking...

152
00:07:08,710 --> 00:07:11,630
speaking of things you do for the rest of your life...

153
00:07:11,630 --> 00:07:15,100
uh, did I read that you're on that-that tenure committee?

154
00:07:15,100 --> 00:07:16,410
Yep.

155
00:07:16,410 --> 00:07:18,900
I got to get a home gym.

156
00:07:19,860 --> 00:07:21,640
Well, I'm sure you have a lot of

157
00:07:21,640 --> 00:07:23,390
good applicants, I just

158
00:07:23,660 --> 00:07:25,950
wanted to say hi and...

159
00:07:25,950 --> 00:07:28,460
let you know that I'll be...

160
00:07:28,460 --> 00:07:30,210
throwing my hat in the...

161
00:07:32,110 --> 00:07:33,520
...ring.

162
00:07:34,220 --> 00:07:36,980
- All right, I'll keep an eye out for that. - Yeah.

163
00:07:36,980 --> 00:07:39,560
Hey, Hofstadter.

164
00:07:39,560 --> 00:07:41,750
Funny seeing you here for the first time in...

165
00:07:41,750 --> 00:07:44,090
ever.

166
00:07:44,870 --> 00:07:47,690
Go... away.

167
00:07:47,910 --> 00:07:49,550
Janine the Machine, let's do this!

168
00:07:49,550 --> 00:07:51,360
Hey, Barry.

169
00:07:52,830 --> 00:07:53,910
Well, look at that.

170
00:07:53,910 --> 00:07:55,950
Burned a whole calorie.

171
00:07:56,490 --> 00:07:58,340
I guess you got here early

172
00:07:58,340 --> 00:08:00,380
to burn off that banana bread I baked you.

173
00:08:00,380 --> 00:08:02,030
Yeah, it was delicious. Thank you.

174
00:08:02,030 --> 00:08:03,120
No, my pleasure.

175
00:08:03,120 --> 00:08:05,620
Oh, if you... if you like banana bread...

176
00:08:05,620 --> 00:08:07,630
I've got a-a great recipe.

177
00:08:07,630 --> 00:08:10,160
The trick is in... in fresh ground...

178
00:08:10,160 --> 00:08:12,410
I'll e-mail it to you.

179
00:08:13,100 --> 00:08:14,540
All right, I'm warm.

180
00:08:14,540 --> 00:08:15,850
Ready to kick it up a notch?

181
00:08:15,850 --> 00:08:16,800
Let's go.

182
00:08:18,430 --> 00:08:20,190
That's enough cardio for me.

183
00:08:20,190 --> 00:08:21,640
I'm just... gonna stretch out

184
00:08:21,640 --> 00:08:23,780
before I hit the weights.

185
00:08:27,670 --> 00:08:29,380
You okay?

186
00:08:30,150 --> 00:08:32,030
Call someone!

187
00:08:37,970 --> 00:08:39,890
Good day, Mrs. Davis.

188
00:08:39,890 --> 00:08:41,790
This is Dr. Rajesh Koothrappali

189
00:08:41,790 --> 00:08:44,240
from the astrophysics department.

190
00:08:44,240 --> 00:08:45,420
Don't be alarmed,

191
00:08:45,420 --> 00:08:48,030
I'm not really in space.

192
00:08:51,280 --> 00:08:53,600
Anyhoo...

193
00:08:53,600 --> 00:08:55,390
I'd like to take this opportunity

194
00:08:55,390 --> 00:08:56,890
to tell you about myself,

195
00:08:56,890 --> 00:08:59,110
so sit back, relax and enjoy

196
00:08:59,110 --> 00:09:01,890
the following 90-minute video.

197
00:09:01,890 --> 00:09:05,320
Oh, come on.

198
00:09:05,320 --> 00:09:06,970
Born in New Delhi,

199
00:09:06,970 --> 00:09:10,360
the third son of an itinerant gynecologist,

200
00:09:10,360 --> 00:09:13,190
I showed early signs of genius.

201
00:09:13,190 --> 00:09:16,580
At the age of five, I discovered a celestial object

202
00:09:16,580 --> 00:09:20,360
which later turned out to be the moon.

203
00:09:22,760 --> 00:09:25,140
Mrs. Davis? Mrs. Davis?

204
00:09:25,140 --> 00:09:26,650
Mrs. Davis?

205
00:09:26,650 --> 00:09:29,250
God, they're everywhere.

206
00:09:29,250 --> 00:09:31,640
Come in.

207
00:09:32,480 --> 00:09:34,320
Dr. Cooper, how can I help you?

208
00:09:34,320 --> 00:09:36,690
Yes, hello. I'm fine. Um...

209
00:09:37,830 --> 00:09:41,170
I understand you may have a bad impression of me,

210
00:09:41,170 --> 00:09:43,120
so I bought you a gift.

211
00:09:43,120 --> 00:09:45,010
Uh, Dr. Cooper, that's not necessary.

212
00:09:45,010 --> 00:09:46,910
It's too late.

213
00:09:47,410 --> 00:09:49,230
Get ready to like me.

214
00:09:52,520 --> 00:09:54,440
Roots?

215
00:09:57,640 --> 00:10:00,170
The tragic history of slavery in America.

216
00:10:00,170 --> 00:10:02,450
Fun for the whole family.

217
00:10:04,220 --> 00:10:07,710
Why would you think this is an appropriate gift?

218
00:10:14,750 --> 00:10:17,080
You are black, right?

219
00:10:19,470 --> 00:10:21,440
This meeting has come to an end.

220
00:10:21,440 --> 00:10:22,990
Because you want to start watching it right now.

221
00:10:22,990 --> 00:10:24,810
Copy that.

222
00:10:26,320 --> 00:10:28,020
Let's see.

223
00:10:28,020 --> 00:10:31,800
Up next on the tenure committee is...

224
00:10:31,800 --> 00:10:33,810
Professor Wu.

225
00:10:33,810 --> 00:10:37,570
Well, get ready for the complete works of Jackie Chan.

226
00:10:44,010 --> 00:10:45,340
Gentlemen.

227
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:49,370
- Where have you been? - Nurse's office.

228
00:10:49,950 --> 00:10:51,070
Asthma attack?

229
00:10:51,070 --> 00:10:52,900
Asthma, heart-- some kind of attack.

230
00:10:52,900 --> 00:10:54,710
I'm fine, though.

231
00:10:55,420 --> 00:10:58,140
You guys going to Professor Tupperman's memorial?

232
00:10:58,140 --> 00:10:59,000
- I don't know. - Probably not.

233
00:10:59,000 --> 00:10:59,860
Barely knew him.

234
00:10:59,860 --> 00:11:00,800
Yeah, you wouldn't want to look like

235
00:11:00,800 --> 00:11:03,470
you guys are brown-nosing the tenure committee,

236
00:11:03,470 --> 00:11:05,100
who will all be there.

237
00:11:07,290 --> 00:11:10,160
Oh, yeah, that's what I was hoping for-- meerkats.

238
00:11:16,860 --> 00:11:20,160
I won't be able to make our date night this Thursday, so...

239
00:11:20,160 --> 00:11:22,170
bad news for you.

240
00:11:23,380 --> 00:11:25,430
You better have a good excuse this time,

241
00:11:25,430 --> 00:11:27,440
because "trimming Q-tips to fit your ears right"

242
00:11:27,440 --> 00:11:29,500
is obvious nonsense.

243
00:11:30,590 --> 00:11:32,220
First of all, when you say things like that,

244
00:11:32,220 --> 00:11:34,100
people think you're crazy.

245
00:11:34,720 --> 00:11:37,190
Second, the reason I'm cancelling is because

246
00:11:37,190 --> 00:11:40,680
I have to attend a memorial service for Professor Tupperman.

247
00:11:40,680 --> 00:11:42,380
It sounds like a long and tedious evening.

248
00:11:42,380 --> 00:11:43,680
Eh, it will be.

249
00:11:43,680 --> 00:11:46,430
Honestly, if I must endure a long and tedious evening,

250
00:11:46,430 --> 00:11:49,290
I'd rather it be with you on date night.

251
00:11:50,570 --> 00:11:51,910
But I have no choice.

252
00:11:51,910 --> 00:11:53,700
The tenure committee's going to be there.

253
00:11:54,640 --> 00:11:57,100
Well, in that case, perhaps I should come along.

254
00:11:57,100 --> 00:11:58,210
Well, now that I think about it,

255
00:11:58,210 --> 00:11:59,630
that would be most helpful.

256
00:11:59,630 --> 00:12:00,730
Of course it would.

257
00:12:00,730 --> 00:12:02,750
I'm well-versed in academic politics,

258
00:12:02,760 --> 00:12:04,360
and as a respected scientist,

259
00:12:04,360 --> 00:12:06,780
I can only raise your stock as a candidate.

260
00:12:06,780 --> 00:12:09,780
Actually, I meant you could drive me.

261
00:12:11,900 --> 00:12:15,270
But if it makes you happy, that other stuff, too.

262
00:12:17,740 --> 00:12:20,830
Do anything interesting today?

263
00:12:20,830 --> 00:12:22,040
Oh, not really.

264
00:12:22,040 --> 00:12:23,650
I was out shopping with Amy.

265
00:12:23,650 --> 00:12:26,790
She wanted me to help her find something for this memorial thing.

266
00:12:26,790 --> 00:12:28,230
Wait-- Sheldon's going to be there?

267
00:12:28,230 --> 00:12:29,950
We all promised we weren't going to go.

268
00:12:29,950 --> 00:12:32,340
- Oh, what a jerk. - I know.

269
00:12:32,340 --> 00:12:35,050
I was hoping to go without anyone finding out.

270
00:12:36,270 --> 00:12:38,720
Well, since Amy's going, do you want me there?

271
00:12:38,720 --> 00:12:39,820
You know, to support you?

272
00:12:40,120 --> 00:12:42,030
That's nice, but it-it's just gonna be

273
00:12:42,030 --> 00:12:43,820
a room full of boring old men

274
